<document lang="en" type="paper">
  <chapter id="ch:linear-regression">
    <title>Linear Regression</title>
    <intro>
      The library implements three linear regression estimators:
      OrdinaryLeastSquares, Ridge and Lasso. They share a prediction rule,
      but use different criteria to estimate its coefficients.
    </intro>
    <section id="linear-model">
      <title>The model and shared interface</title>
      For $X\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times p}$ and $y\in\mathbb{R}^n$, the prediction is
      <equation>\hat y_i=\beta_0+\sum_{j=1}^p\beta_jx_{ij}.</equation>
      Instantiate an estimator without arguments, then call fit with a
      two-dimensional feature matrix and a one-dimensional target array.
      All three fit methods return None. After fitting, <c>coef</c> contains
      the $p$ slope coefficients and <c>intercept</c> contains the scalar intercept.
      Accessing these properties before fitting raises RuntimeError.
      <c>predict(X)</c> returns one prediction per row, using the same feature
      order and count as training. Do not add a constant column when intercept=True.
      The shared methods <c>RSS(X, y)</c>, <c>MSE(X, y)</c>,
      <c>RMSE(X, y)</c> and <c>R2(X, y)</c> evaluate predictions against targets.
      R2 raises ValueError when the targets are constant.
    </section>
    <section id="linear-ols">
      <title>Ordinary least squares</title>
      OrdinaryLeastSquares minimizes the residual sum of squares:
      <equation>\operatorname{RSS}=\sum_{i=1}^n(y_i-\hat y_i)^2.</equation>
      <c>fit(X, y, intercept=True)</c> optionally adds a column of ones.
      If $A$ denotes this augmented matrix, or $X$ when no intercept is fitted,
      the coefficients solve the normal equations
      <equation>A^\top A\hat\beta=A^\top y.</equation>
      The implementation uses numpy.linalg.solve and requires full column rank
      of $A$, including the intercept column. Otherwise it raises ValueError.
      With intercept=False, the fitted intercept is zero.
      <proof collapsed="true">
        Differentiating $\|y-A\beta\|_2^2$ gives
        $-2A^\top(y-A\beta)$. At a minimum this gradient vanishes,
        yielding the normal equations. Full column rank makes $A^\top A$
        positive definite, so the solution is unique.
      </proof>
    </section>
    <section id="linear-scaling">
      <title>Scaling in Ridge and Lasso</title>
      Both regularized estimators standardize the features and the target
      internally, using training means and population standard deviations.
      Zero standard deviations are replaced by one. Write $Z$ and $t$ for
      these standardized arrays, and $b$ for their fitted coefficients.
      The penalties below apply to these standardized coefficients, not directly
      to the coefficients in the original units.
      <equation>\beta_j=\frac{s_y}{s_j}b_j,\qquad
        \beta_0=\bar y-\sum_j\bar x_j\beta_j.</equation>
      The intercept is not penalized. The recovered coefficients allow predict
      to accept inputs in their original units.
      In the current implementation, intercept=False still centers X and y
      internally but forces the final intercept to zero. On uncentered data,
      this differs from solving the usual regression problem constrained to
      pass through the origin. Use the default intercept=True unless that
      behavior is specifically intended.
    </section>
    <section id="linear-ridge">
      <title>Ridge</title>
      Ridge adds a squared coefficient penalty:
      <equation>\min_b\;\|t-Zb\|_2^2+\lambda\sum_{j=1}^p b_j^2.</equation>
      <c>fit(X, y, lam, intercept=True)</c> requires a nonnegative lam.
      It solves
      <equation>(Z^\top Z+\lambda I)b=Z^\top t.</equation>
      A positive lambda makes this matrix positive definite even when features
      are linearly dependent. With lam=0, a singular system can still fail.
      Larger penalties shrink the standardized coefficients toward zero,
      trading flexibility for stability.
    </section>
    <section id="linear-lasso">
      <title>Lasso</title>
      Lasso uses an absolute coefficient penalty:
      <equation>\min_b\;\|t-Zb\|_2^2+\lambda\sum_{j=1}^p|b_j|.</equation>
      <c>fit(X, y, lam, intercept=True, eps=1e-4, max_iter=10000)</c>
      requires nonnegative lam and eps and a positive integer iteration limit.
      Unlike Ridge, the general problem has no single normal-equation formula.
      The implementation uses coordinate descent, updating one coefficient
      at a time by soft thresholding rather than an ordinary gradient step.
      For a nonconstant standardized column $Z_j$, define
      <equations>
        r_j&=t-Zb+Z_jb_j,\\
        z_j&=Z_j^\top r_j,\\
        b_j&=\frac{\operatorname{sign}(z_j)\max(|z_j|-\lambda/2,0)}{n}.
      </equations>
      Here $Z_j^\top Z_j=n$ because population standard deviations are used.
      The threshold can make coefficients exactly zero. A constant centered
      column is zero and receives a zero coefficient.
      <proof collapsed="true">
        Holding the other coefficients fixed leaves the one-dimensional problem
        $\|r_j-Z_ju\|_2^2+\lambda|u|$.
        For $u>0$ its derivative is $2nu-2z_j+\lambda$;
        for $u<0$ it is $2nu-2z_j-\lambda$.
        At zero, the subgradient contains zero exactly when
        $|z_j|\le\lambda/2$. These cases yield the update above.
      </proof>
      The stopping criterion compares the sum of absolute coefficient changes
      between full sweeps with eps. The iteration counter advances per coordinate,
      but the limit is checked between sweeps, so the last sweep can exceed
      max_iter slightly. Reaching the limit does not itself certify convergence.
    </section>
    <section id="linear-example">
      <title>Example</title>
      <code lang="python" numbered="false">
import numpy as np
from ml_library import OrdinaryLeastSquares, Ridge, Lasso

X = np.array([[1., -1.], [2., 5.], [0., -2.], [-3., 2.], [3., 4.]])
y = np.array([1., 2., 3., 4., 5.])
new_points = np.array([[0., 0.], [1., 2.]])

ols = OrdinaryLeastSquares()
ols.fit(X, y)
ridge = Ridge()
ridge.fit(X, y, lam=1.0)
lasso = Lasso()
lasso.fit(X, y, lam=0.1, eps=1e-4, max_iter=10000)

for model in (ols, ridge, lasso):
    print(type(model).__name__)
    print(model.intercept, model.coef)
    print(model.predict(new_points))
      </code>
      Choose lam using validation data rather than the final test set.
      The objectives use a sum of squared errors, not its average; penalty
      values are therefore not directly interchangeable with implementations
      using a different normalization. For polynomial features, see
      <link to="polynomial-regression.html">Polynomial Regression</link>.
    </section>
  </chapter>
</document>
